\ Cutlet partition by positive gate-span lengths in lexicographic order.
\ The total is G spans, and the number of cutlets is K.
\ A partition is (x1,...,xK), xi positive, sum xi=G.
\ If the calculation day is an internal gate at offset q, at least
\ one partial sum must be exactly q. Here q=0 means no restriction.
\
\ Counting is exact, with no enumeration of the potentially enormous
\ set of partitions. Each partition corresponds to choosing K-1 cuts
\ among G-1 internal gate boundaries. If a fixed cut q is required,
\ there are C(G-2,K-2) instead of C(G-1,K-1) choices.
\ Lexicographic unranking considers candidate first lengths ascending,
\ counting the complete suffix before moving to the next candidate.
\ All counts and ranks use decimal wide quantities.

A cutlet piece is a thing with
  a number called span length.

The chosen cutlet pieces are some cutlet pieces.
The new cutlet piece is a cutlet piece.

A cutlet partition has
  a number called total spans,
  a number called part count,
  a number called required boundary,
  a number called remaining spans,
  a number called remaining parts,
  a number called remaining boundary,
  a number called suffix spans,
  a number called suffix parts,
  a number called candidate length,
  a number called part chosen,
  a wide quantity called total choices,
  a wide quantity called suffix choices,
  a wide quantity called current rank,
  a number called valid selection.

The cutlet partition is a cutlet partition.

\ Find a gate coinciding with c strictly inside the selected year.
\ G_0 is at the chosen year's opening index; the closing index cannot
\ be internal. Scan one gate at a time to avoid repeated full gate walks.
\ This scan uses the sauce and must precede the year-structure sauce.
To find the restricted internal cutlet boundary:
  Put 0 into the cutlet partition's required boundary.
  Put the chosen year's opening index into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the chosen year's opening day into the gate walk's current position.
  Loop.
    Put the gate walk's current index into a following index number.
    Add 1 to the following index number.
    If the following index number is greater than or equal to the chosen year's closing index, break.
    Move to the next positive gate.
    If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
    If the gate walk's current position is the year selection day,
      put the gate walk's current index into the cutlet partition's required boundary;
      subtract the chosen year's opening index from the cutlet partition's required boundary;
      break.
    If the gate walk's current position is greater than the year selection day, break.
  Repeat.

\ Calculate the total number of permitted partitions, optionally
\ requiring the one distinguished internal gate boundary.
To count the permitted cutlet partitions:
  Put "0" into the cutlet partition's total choices.
  If the cutlet partition's part count is less than 1, exit.
  If the cutlet partition's part count is greater than the cutlet partition's total spans, exit.
  If the cutlet partition's required boundary is greater than 0,
    count cutlet partitions with required boundaries; exit.
  Put the cutlet partition's total spans into a first binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the first binomial number.
  Put the cutlet partition's part count into a second binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the second binomial number.
  Calculate binomial coefficients given the first binomial number and the second binomial number.
  Put the binomial result into the cutlet partition's total choices.

\ One fixed interior cut uses one of the K-1 interior boundaries.
To count cutlet partitions with required boundaries:
  If the cutlet partition's required boundary is greater than or equal to the cutlet partition's total spans, exit.
  If the cutlet partition's part count is less than 2, exit.
  Put the cutlet partition's total spans into a first binomial number.
  Subtract 2 from the first binomial number.
  Put the cutlet partition's part count into a second binomial number.
  Subtract 2 from the second binomial number.
  Calculate binomial coefficients given the first binomial number and the second binomial number.
  Put the binomial result into the cutlet partition's total choices.

\ Count suffixes if we take candidate length x at the current stage.
\ If the required cut is after x, one interior suffix cut is fixed:
\ C((R-x)-2,(P-1)-2). If x equals the cut, it is satisfied:
\ C((R-x)-1,(P-1)-1). If there is no pin, use the latter.
To count the current cutlet suffix:
  Put "0" into the cutlet partition's suffix choices.
  Put the cutlet partition's remaining spans into the cutlet partition's suffix spans.
  Subtract the cutlet partition's candidate length from the cutlet partition's suffix spans.
  Put the cutlet partition's remaining parts into the cutlet partition's suffix parts.
  Subtract 1 from the cutlet partition's suffix parts.
  If the cutlet partition's suffix parts is 0,
    count the last cutlet suffix; exit.
  If the cutlet partition's remaining boundary is greater than 0,
    count restricted cutlet suffixes; exit.
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix spans into a first binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the first binomial number.
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix parts into a second binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the second binomial number.
  Calculate binomial coefficients given the first binomial number and the second binomial number.
  Put the binomial result into the cutlet partition's suffix choices.

To count the last cutlet suffix:
  If the cutlet partition's suffix spans is not 0, exit.
  If the cutlet partition's remaining boundary is greater than 0, exit.
  Put "1" into the cutlet partition's suffix choices.

To count restricted cutlet suffixes:
  If the cutlet partition's candidate length is greater than the cutlet partition's remaining boundary, exit.
  If the cutlet partition's candidate length is the cutlet partition's remaining boundary,
    count satisfied cutlet suffixes; exit.
  If the cutlet partition's suffix parts is less than 2, exit.
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix spans into a first binomial number.
  Subtract 2 from the first binomial number.
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix parts into a second binomial number.
  Subtract 2 from the second binomial number.
  Calculate binomial coefficients given the first binomial number and the second binomial number.
  Put the binomial result into the cutlet partition's suffix choices.

To count satisfied cutlet suffixes:
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix spans into a first binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the first binomial number.
  Put the cutlet partition's suffix parts into a second binomial number.
  Subtract 1 from the second binomial number.
  Calculate binomial coefficients given the first binomial number and the second binomial number.
  Put the binomial result into the cutlet partition's suffix choices.

\ Append one selected part to the permanent composition result.
To remember the selected cutlet length:
  Allocate memory for the new cutlet piece.
  Put the cutlet partition's candidate length into the new cutlet piece's span length.
  Append the new cutlet piece to the chosen cutlet pieces.
  Subtract the cutlet partition's candidate length from the cutlet partition's remaining spans.
  Subtract 1 from the cutlet partition's remaining parts.
  If the cutlet partition's remaining boundary is greater than 0,
    update the remaining cutlet boundary.

To update the remaining cutlet boundary:
  If the cutlet partition's candidate length is the cutlet partition's remaining boundary,
    put 0 into the cutlet partition's remaining boundary; exit.
  Subtract the cutlet partition's candidate length from the cutlet partition's remaining boundary.

\ Try each positive length in ascending order, skipping its entire
\ block if its count is smaller than the desired one-based rank.
To choose the next cutlet length:
  Put 0 into the cutlet partition's part chosen.
  Put 1 into the cutlet partition's candidate length.
  Loop.
    Put the cutlet partition's remaining spans into a maximum length number.
    Subtract the cutlet partition's remaining parts from the maximum length number.
    Add 1 to the maximum length number.
    If the cutlet partition's candidate length is greater than the maximum length number, break.
    Count the current cutlet suffix.
    If the cutlet partition's suffix choices is "0",
      add 1 to the cutlet partition's candidate length.
    If the cutlet partition's suffix choices is not "0",
      consider the current cutlet length.
    If the cutlet partition's part chosen is 1, break.
  Repeat.

To consider the current cutlet length:
  If the cutlet partition's suffix choices is numerically smaller than the cutlet partition's current rank,
    subtract the cutlet partition's suffix choices from the cutlet partition's current rank;
    add 1 to the cutlet partition's candidate length;
    exit.
  Remember the selected cutlet length.
  Put 1 into the cutlet partition's part chosen.

\ The candidate-length loop finishes when one candidate is selected.
\ All parts are determined by arithmetic counts, not random guessing.
To select a cutlet partition from a wide quantity:
  Put 0 into the cutlet partition's valid selection.
  Destroy the chosen cutlet pieces.
  Put the wide quantity into the cutlet partition's current rank.
  If the cutlet partition's current rank is "0", exit.
  Count the permitted cutlet partitions.
  If the cutlet partition's total choices is "0", exit.
  If the cutlet partition's total choices is numerically smaller than the cutlet partition's current rank, exit.
  Put the cutlet partition's total spans into the cutlet partition's remaining spans.
  Put the cutlet partition's part count into the cutlet partition's remaining parts.
  Put the cutlet partition's required boundary into the cutlet partition's remaining boundary.
  Loop.
    If the cutlet partition's remaining parts is 0, break.
    Choose the next cutlet length.
    If the cutlet partition's part chosen is not 1, exit.
  Repeat.
  Put 1 into the cutlet partition's valid selection.

\ The structural sauce was prepared after the internal gate scan.
\ Bowl 2 with seal 21 selects an exact one-based composition rank.
To choose the canonical cutlet partition:
  Put the chosen year's span count into the cutlet partition's total spans.
  Put the selected cutlet count into the cutlet partition's part count.
  Count the permitted cutlet partitions.
  If the cutlet partition's total choices is "0", exit.
  Put 2 into the seal inquiry's bowl identity.
  Put 21 into the seal inquiry's seal number.
  Begin a seal inquiry.
  Determine canonical equal selection given the cutlet partition's total choices.
  Select a cutlet partition from the seal inquiry's selected quantity.


\ This wrapper does the gate scan BEFORE calculating the shared year sauce.
\ The scan would otherwise overwrite the bowls needed by seals 20 and 21.
To choose the cutlet structure for the selected year:
  Find the restricted internal cutlet boundary.
  Choose the structural counts.
  Choose the canonical cutlet partition.
